@table
Fertiliser | Nutrient content | Note
**Urea**, CO(NH_{2})_{2} | **46% N** (amide form) | **Organic** compound (first made by Wöhler, 1828); most soluble, used for **foliar spray**; about **2.2 kg** gives 1 kg N; **Nano urea** (IFFCO, 2021) is a liquid spray
**DAP** (diammonium phosphate) | **18% N + 46% P_{2}O_{5}** | Second most used in India
**Ammonium sulphate** | 21% N + 24% S | Leaves the **most acidity** in soil
Calcium ammonium nitrate | 25-26% N | Neutral
**MOP** (muriate of potash, KCl) | 60% K_{2}O | Almost all imported
Single superphosphate | 16% P_{2}O_{5} | Also supplies sulphur and calcium
@end

Plants get **carbon, hydrogen and oxygen** from air and water, not from fertilisers. Organic manure supplies only a few kilograms of phosphorus per tonne, so chemical phosphate fertilisers are needed. India's ideal **N : P : K ratio is 4 : 2 : 1**, but actual use is much richer in nitrogen.

@section 19.5 | Pesticides and Agricultural Chemicals

@q 32:2-8

@qnote 32:7 | **Aluminium phosphide** (celphos) releases deadly phosphine gas. It is used chiefly as a **fumigant** for stored grain, and so also acts as an insecticide and rodenticide in godowns; with several right answers, the source marks the question (*).

**Pyrethrum** (from the dried flowers of *Chrysanthemum cinerariaefolium*, a seed plant) gives **pyrethrins**, the natural insecticide in mosquito coils. **Zinc phosphide** is the common **rat poison**. **Sodium chlorate** is a weedkiller (herbicide).

