Reason (R) : Dry cell has a reversible reaction.
Code :
(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).
(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).
(c) (A) is true, but (R) is false.
(d) (A) is false, but (R) is true.
@ans (c)
@end

@q 32:35

An ordinary dry (Leclanché) cell is a **primary cell**: its reaction is **irreversible**, so it cannot be recharged. Lead-acid, nickel-cadmium and **lithium-ion** cells are secondary (rechargeable) cells.

@section 19.8 | Miscellany

@q 32:1

**Ozone** disinfects water because it is a strong **oxidising agent** that destroys microbes; it is not radioactive, but that is not why it works.

@q 32:10-11

**Radiometric dating** measures the age of rocks from the decay of long-lived isotopes such as **uranium → lead** (half-life 4.5 billion years) and potassium → argon; this gives the Earth's age of about **4.54 billion years**. **Carbon-14** (half-life 5,730 years) dates once-living material up to about 50,000 years old.

@q 32:27

@qnote 32:27 | The source gives **(a) & (d)**: India's first **low-temperature thermal desalination** plant was set up at **Kavaratti, Lakshadweep** (2005), and Chennai has large sea-water reverse-osmosis plants (Minjur, 2010; Nemmeli).

@q 32:31-33

**Mustard gas** (sulphur mustard) is a blistering chemical weapon first used in World War I; the **Chemical Weapons Convention** (1993) bans it. Smoke screens disperse fine particles of **titanium dioxide** (or zinc chloride, phosphorus smoke) in air.

